      card(
        title: [Conclusion & Outlook],
      )[
        *#toolname* is an open-source EEG dementia classification assistant that makes the complete inference pipeline of the xEEGNet dementia classifier interactively explorable - from raw EEG signals to per-band spectral features, spatial topomaps, and signed class contributions.
        By grounding every visual element in the model's *architecture-intrinsic quantities*, #toolname provides a transparency that post-hoc attribution methods cannot match.

        *Physiological Motivation*: xEEGNet's architecture is motivated by medical insights, e.g. the fixed bandpass filters are well-known features in EEG literature.
        Lower relative *$alpha$-band power*, for example, is a *known dementia indicator*.
        For better reference, we also allow the user to compare a patient's data to the cohort distribution.

        *Central Model Claim*: Architecture-intrinsic explainability beats post-hoc attribution: clinicians can inspect *real evidence*, not saliency maps.
        Interviews with an experienced neurologist confirm the pipeline matches clinical reasoning, especially for healthy-vs.-dementia screening.

        *Voice Control Integration*: The natural-language interface is an accessibility highlight, non-expert users can navigate the tool easily, even by voice interaction.
        A case for no-hands interaction in the lab could also be made.

        *Future Work* includes counterfactuals, model extensions and new model architectures, a broader usability study and additional EEG datasets.

